#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84e45ca-c250-5e65-a04e-4714a485f38e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Determinant of a Triangular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6a0257ef-3ec6-501c-bbf7-1c1108d4f8b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10991" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 10 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &= (-2) \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\cdot (-2) = 4\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros"}] :question/id #uuid "c9760010-bcdd-53f4-8428-a3f5ecdd838b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10992" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 19 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $1$st column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-1)^{1 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-3) =-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 2 \\cdot (-6) = -12\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/id #uuid "1a6556a1-22b3-5e9b-97aa-d3e644df9f56" :question/math-academy-id "e-10993" :question/problem "Given that $\\det(A)=4$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{det}(A^T)=16$\n2. $3\\textrm{det}(A^T)=12$\n3. $\\textrm{det}(A^T)+\\textrm{det}(I)=17$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A^T)=\\textrm{det}(A)=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(I)=1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Rather,\n$\\textrm{det}(A^T)=4 \\neq 16$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$3\\textrm{det}(A^T)= 3 \\cdot 4 = 12$.\n- Statement III is false. We have\n$\\textrm{det}(A^T)+\\textrm{det}(I)= 4 + 1 = 5 \\neq 17$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3118d2af-771f-54c9-b18e-5196b87b6dca" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of an N-Dimensional Parallelepiped"}] :question/id #uuid "a67e620f-6380-5754-81db-7e593cdf4336" :question/math-academy-id "e-10998" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M$, whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, $\\mathbf{c}$, and $\\mathbf{d}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\mid \\det(M) \\mid\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(M) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3 \\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(M) &= -3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3(15 + 4) \\\\\n&=-57\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV = \\begin{vmatrix}\\det(M) & = & -57\\end{vmatrix} = 57\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c7a045-2a9c-5b28-93f2-d5bf8c28422d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118600} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d2ddb9-985d-5550-a1b8-a62fb7d1b923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118601} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b7586c-733b-5b30-97e2-71f5b1cf06f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118602} {:answer/id #uuid "259650bd-f7d9-5880-a733-f480f75a8314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118603} {:answer/id #uuid "34714c07-3f74-59e0-8299-f64b889f7d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d2ddb9-985d-5550-a1b8-a62fb7d1b923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118601} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4749bf9-c2b2-59b3-8011-065f167070ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118599}] :question/id #uuid "8d9feede-df40-51d8-ab1e-cf33d9bd1f0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42468" :question/problem "Given that $det(A^{T}) = 5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $4det(A) = 20$\n2. $2det(A^{T}) = 25$\n3. $det(A) - 3det(I) = 8$"}]}
